Ze patch to the Go standard library: runtime tracebackHexdump returns when
frame.sp is not on the stack it was given.

How this file is applied, reviewed and replaced on a Go upgrade:
docs/contributing/patching-the-standard-library.md

The defect, read in go1.27.3 and unchanged on golang/go master (2026-10-25).
racecall (runtime/race_arm64.s) moves SP to g0's stack and calls the race
runtime without changing g. A SIGQUIT that lands there makes sighandler
trace the user goroutine from a PC inside C code, so (*unwinder).initAt
prints "unknown pc" and calls tracebackHexdump(gp.stack, ...). frame.sp is
on g0's stack, so clamping to gp.stack leaves hi below lo, and
hexdumpWords(lo, hi-lo, ...) receives a wrapped length: "fatal error:
unsafe.Slice: len out of range", then "panic during panic". The second
traceback (fatalthrow, dopanic_m, tracebackothers) re-traces the same
goroutine from its saved registers. On darwin/arm64 every sample of the hung
process sits on the load at traceback.go:374 in (*unwinder).resolveInternal,
and the dump stops after its first goroutine; that the load faults and is
re-executed while the handler blocks SIGSEGV is inferred, not traced. Before CL 681375 ("runtime: add hexdumper", go1.26),
hexdumpWords took an end address and printed nothing when end <= p; the
hunk below restores that outcome.

Upstream status: not yet reported. The same output appears in builder
failures on golang/go#73470 (os/exec TestWaitInterrupt/SIGQUIT, linux msan
and asan), which names no cause. Delete this patch when the toolchain Ze
pins carries the fix.

No Ze code references this patch: a build without it compiles and runs
the same, and only a SIGQUIT dump under -race (or msan, asan) differs.

Ze-Reviewed-Against: go1.27.3

diff --git a/src/runtime/traceback.go b/src/runtime/traceback.go
index 58161de..0fa83be 100644
--- a/src/runtime/traceback.go
+++ b/src/runtime/traceback.go
@@ -1462,6 +1462,13 @@ func tracebackHexdump(stk stack, frame *stkframe, bad uintptr) {

 	// Print the hex dump.
 	print("stack: frame={sp:", hex(frame.sp), ", fp:", hex(frame.fp), "} stack=[", hex(stk.lo), ",", hex(stk.hi), ")\n")
+	if hi <= lo {
+		// frame.sp is not on stk, so the clamped range is empty or
+		// inverted and hi-lo would wrap to a huge length. A signal
+		// that lands while gp runs C code on the system stack without
+		// switching g, as racecall does, gets here.
+		return
+	}
 	hexdumpWords(lo, hi-lo, func(p uintptr, m hexdumpMarker) {
 		if p == frame.fp {
 			m.start()
